  "worked_solution": "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $m,\\,6m,$ and $12m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,6m,12m)=12m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m} & =\\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12m(\\frac{3}{m}+\\frac{4+m}{6m}) & =12m(\\frac{5}{12m}) \\\\ 12m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+12m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot m\\cdot \\frac{3}{m}+2\\cdot 6m\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{6m} & =12m\\cdot \\frac{5}{12m} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}}+2\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot \\frac{4+m}{\\cancel{6m}} & =\\cancel{12m}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{12m}} \\\\ 36+2(4+m) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36+2(4+m) & =5 \\\\ 36+8+2m & =5 \\\\ 44+2m & =5 \\\\ 2m & =-39 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{39}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. However, since $m=-\\frac{39}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{39}{2}.$",
